1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,700
Fort Palmer, Fort Canning and the
Forts That Guarded a Free Port

2
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:08,902
On the afternoon of the 13th
of October 1891, a party of

3
00:00:08,982 --> 00:00:13,414
ladies and gentlemen climbed
Mount Wallich, above the docks

4
00:00:13,494 --> 00:00:17,700
at Tanjong Pagar, to watch
the Governor press a button.

5
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:22,243
An electric wire ran from the hilltop
to two new 10-inch breech-loading

6
00:00:22,323 --> 00:00:26,410
guns in the fort on neighbouring
Mount Palmer, the heaviest guns

7
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:30,838
in any fortification in the Straits
Settlements, and when he pressed

8
00:00:30,918 --> 00:00:35,150
it they fired at a target boat
three and a half miles out to sea.

9
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:38,926
Seven months earlier, at what
the Straits Times called the

10
00:00:39,006 --> 00:00:41,652
largest gathering ever held in Singapore,

11
00:00:41,732 --> 00:00:46,638
the town's merchants had protested
against the sum London wanted the colony

12
00:00:46,718 --> 00:00:50,375
to pay each year for the
soldiers who manned such guns.

13
00:00:50,375 --> 00:00:54,417
A port that charged no duties
on its trade still needed forts,

14
00:00:54,497 --> 00:00:58,273
and for most of the nineteenth
century the question of who

15
00:00:58,353 --> 00:01:02,675
should pay for them was argued
over as hard as where to put them.

16
00:01:02,675 --> 00:01:06,875
The Straits Times described
the firing the next morning.

17
00:01:06,875 --> 00:01:10,195
The two guns had arrived
from England several months

18
00:01:10,275 --> 00:01:13,857
before and had been mounted
in the fort at Mount Palmer,

19
00:01:13,937 --> 00:01:17,075
overlooking the eastern
entrance to the harbour.

20
00:01:17,075 --> 00:01:20,938
The military authorities
treated their first firing

21
00:01:21,018 --> 00:01:24,575
as a public occasion and
sent out invitations.

22
00:01:24,575 --> 00:01:27,864
According to the paper,
the Governor also took the

23
00:01:27,944 --> 00:01:31,367
trouble to reassure the
ladies in the party that the

24
00:01:31,447 --> 00:01:34,950
concussion was unlikely to
set off another landslip.

25
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:38,759
The target was a small
tongkang, a local cargo boat,

26
00:01:38,839 --> 00:01:44,000
rigged with red and white bunting
and anchored about 6,000 yards out.

27
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,431
The Governor pressed the button and the

28
00:01:46,511 --> 00:01:49,150
first shot fell in a good line but short.

29
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:53,577
The target for the second shot
was about 11,300 yards away,

30
00:01:53,657 --> 00:01:57,932
between six and six and a half
miles, and a large Chinese

31
00:01:58,012 --> 00:02:02,825
tongkang was sailing "quite
unconcernedly" near it at the time.

32
00:02:02,825 --> 00:02:06,916
The shell struck the water not
far from the boat, and the paper

33
00:02:06,996 --> 00:02:11,100
reported that it caused "no end
of consternation" to the crew.

34
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:15,537
At the shorter range, the paper
said, a shell took 12 seconds to

35
00:02:15,617 --> 00:02:20,275
reach the water and could pierce
eleven and a half inches of iron.

36
00:02:20,275 --> 00:02:25,200
The new guns had a cost the visitors
could not see from the hilltop.

37
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,411
Later that month the Straits
Times reported that the concussion

38
00:02:29,491 --> 00:02:32,681
from the gun practice had
made the Parsee Lodge,

39
00:02:32,761 --> 00:02:37,325
an old building on the western side
of Mount Palmer, uninhabitable.

40
00:02:37,325 --> 00:02:40,925
Singapore's defences began modestly.

41
00:02:40,925 --> 00:02:43,777
Soon after the treaty of February 1819,

42
00:02:43,857 --> 00:02:47,761
Stamford Raffles told the
Resident, William Farquhar,

43
00:02:47,841 --> 00:02:52,050
to put up "one or two strong
batteries" along the coast.

44
00:02:52,050 --> 00:02:55,325
For years that was close to all there was.

45
00:02:55,325 --> 00:02:58,733
Singapore was a free port
that charged no duties

46
00:02:58,813 --> 00:03:02,366
on the goods passing through
it, so its government

47
00:03:02,446 --> 00:03:05,425
had no customs revenue to spend on forts.

48
00:03:05,425 --> 00:03:09,750
The money came instead from revenue
farms, licences to sell opium,

49
00:03:09,830 --> 00:03:14,221
spirits and other goods, and at
its peak in the nineteenth century,

50
00:03:14,301 --> 00:03:16,890
according to the National Library Board,

51
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:21,575
opium accounted for over half the
revenue of the Straits Settlements.

52
00:03:21,575 --> 00:03:26,524
The first full survey of the
defences was made in 1827 by Captain

53
00:03:26,604 --> 00:03:31,321
Edward Lake of the Bengal
Engineers, who found them scanty and

54
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:36,275
recommended a chain of batteries
along the coast and a citadel.

55
00:03:36,275 --> 00:03:39,219
He picked out two hills, Mount Palmer near

56
00:03:39,299 --> 00:03:42,900
Tanjong Pagar and Pearl's
Hill, as sites for guns.

57
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:47,395
The only fort that came out of
his report was Fort Fullerton,

58
00:03:47,475 --> 00:03:52,345
at the mouth of the Singapore
River, probably begun under Governor

59
00:03:52,425 --> 00:03:56,850
Robert Fullerton between 1826
and 1830 and named after him.

60
00:03:56,850 --> 00:04:02,182
Between 1854 and 1859 it was
enlarged to three times its size,

61
00:04:02,262 --> 00:04:06,808
and by 1858 it mounted seven
68-pounders with a range

62
00:04:06,888 --> 00:04:11,950
of about three miles, along
with smaller guns and mortars.

63
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:14,875
The merchants did not much like it.

64
00:04:14,875 --> 00:04:18,540
The fort sat on what they
saw as the best site in town,

65
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:21,127
its artillery practice was a nuisance,

66
00:04:21,207 --> 00:04:25,144
and their godowns along the
river and at Commercial Square,

67
00:04:25,224 --> 00:04:28,900
now Raffles Place, stood
directly in its line of fire.

68
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:32,973
In the 1850s a visiting
naval captain said his ship's

69
00:04:33,053 --> 00:04:37,050
guns could "smash the fort
in a minute and a half".

70
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:40,470
By 1864 a second battery, Fort Palmer,

71
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:45,800
stood on part of Mount Palmer,
with five 56-pounder guns.

72
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:50,226
In 1859 the colonial government
pulled down Government House,

73
00:04:50,306 --> 00:04:56,801
the governor's residence on the hill above
the town that had begun as Raffles's bungalow,

74
00:04:56,881 --> 00:05:00,575
and began building an
artillery fort in its place.

75
00:05:00,575 --> 00:05:03,169
Some people objected at the time that

76
00:05:03,249 --> 00:05:05,850
the hill was too far from the shore.

77
00:05:05,850 --> 00:05:10,940
About 400 Chinese labourers
cut a plateau into the hilltop,

78
00:05:11,020 --> 00:05:14,000
and the fort was finished in 1861.

79
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,592
It was named Fort Canning,
after Viscount Canning,

80
00:05:17,672 --> 00:05:19,875
the Governor-General of India.

81
00:05:19,875 --> 00:05:23,824
Seven 68-pounders were in place facing the

82
00:05:23,904 --> 00:05:28,045
sea by May 1859, and
eight 8-inch shell guns

83
00:05:28,125 --> 00:05:32,250
and two 13-inch mortars
were added in 1867.

84
00:05:32,250 --> 00:05:35,792
The fort was also meant as
a place of refuge in case of

85
00:05:35,872 --> 00:05:38,953
trouble in the town, but
it had no water supply,

86
00:05:39,033 --> 00:05:43,432
which made it useless for that, and
ships could come close enough to

87
00:05:43,512 --> 00:05:47,200
bombard the town while staying
out of range of its guns.

88
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:51,900
Its most regular duty turned
out to be telling the time.

89
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:55,554
A 68-pounder was fired
at five o'clock every

90
00:05:55,634 --> 00:05:59,712
morning to start the day,
and until 1896 the guns

91
00:05:59,792 --> 00:06:03,950
were also fired to signal
the outbreak of a fire.

92
00:06:03,950 --> 00:06:07,322
When the fort was finished,
the engineers found that

93
00:06:07,402 --> 00:06:11,239
the battery on Pearl's Hill
stood higher than Fort Canning,

94
00:06:11,319 --> 00:06:13,975
so the top of Pearl's Hill was cut down.

95
00:06:13,975 --> 00:06:17,718
Until 1867 the Straits
Settlements were governed

96
00:06:17,798 --> 00:06:21,222
from British India, and
in Singapore the old

97
00:06:21,302 --> 00:06:25,125
defences came to be seen
as a relic of that era.

98
00:06:25,125 --> 00:06:28,243
Fort Fullerton was dismantled

99
00:06:28,323 --> 00:06:31,300
between the 1860s and 1873.

100
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:35,468
An 1872 editorial in the Straits
Times Overland Journal said

101
00:06:35,548 --> 00:06:39,574
the fort seemed "expressly
designed for the destruction of

102
00:06:39,654 --> 00:06:43,681
the Collyer Quay godowns and
the whole Commercial Square",

103
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:48,150
and suggested that the site would
suit the Post Office better.

104
00:06:48,150 --> 00:06:52,600
The Post Office moved
there in August 1874.

105
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:55,675
What brought new forts was the harbour.

106
00:06:55,675 --> 00:06:59,275
After the Suez Canal opened
in 1869, more and more

107
00:06:59,355 --> 00:07:03,249
steamships called at Singapore,
and the docks and coal

108
00:07:03,329 --> 00:07:07,370
stores of the Tanjong Pagar
Dock Company at New Harbour,

109
00:07:07,450 --> 00:07:11,350
later renamed Keppel Harbour,
became worth defending.

110
00:07:11,350 --> 00:07:14,620
From 1878, when Britain
feared a war with Russia,

111
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:19,406
forts were built on both sides of
the western entrance to the harbour:

112
00:07:19,486 --> 00:07:23,644
Fort Pasir Panjang on the mainland,
now part of Labrador Park,

113
00:07:23,724 --> 00:07:27,131
and Fort Siloso on Pulau
Blakang Mati, now Sentosa,

114
00:07:27,211 --> 00:07:31,450
with Fort Serapong and Fort
Connaught elsewhere on the island.

115
00:07:31,450 --> 00:07:36,075
The batteries on Mount Palmer
were rebuilt at the same time.

116
00:07:36,075 --> 00:07:39,953
The Dock Company owned the hill,
and under a deal with Governor

117
00:07:40,033 --> 00:07:43,912
William Robinson it let the
government put a battery on the top

118
00:07:43,992 --> 00:07:47,950
in return for the government
paying for the roads to the docks.

119
00:07:47,950 --> 00:07:52,450
The hilltop was handed over in 1879.

120
00:07:52,450 --> 00:07:56,531
The forts were manned by the
garrison, British regular troops

121
00:07:56,611 --> 00:08:00,693
such as the Royal Artillery,
and the Straits Settlements paid

122
00:08:00,773 --> 00:08:05,275
London a yearly military contribution
towards the garrison's cost.

123
00:08:05,275 --> 00:08:09,493
In 1890 the Colonial
Office asked the colony for

124
00:08:09,573 --> 00:08:13,612
£60,000 to build new
barracks and other works,

125
00:08:13,692 --> 00:08:18,975
and proposed to double the
annual contribution to £100,000.

126
00:08:18,975 --> 00:08:23,414
The Singapore branch of the
Straits Settlements Association,

127
00:08:23,494 --> 00:08:26,804
the merchants' lobby,
called a public meeting

128
00:08:26,884 --> 00:08:30,725
at the Town Hall for Friday
the 13th of March 1891.

129
00:08:30,725 --> 00:08:34,725
The Straits Times gave a
whole page to it the next day.

130
00:08:34,725 --> 00:08:37,857
According to the paper,
social engagements were put

131
00:08:37,937 --> 00:08:41,383
off so that people could
attend, crowds gathered outside

132
00:08:41,463 --> 00:08:44,154
the hall well before
the five o'clock start,

133
00:08:44,234 --> 00:08:48,075
and the verandahs and galleries
were "filled to overflowing".

134
00:08:48,075 --> 00:08:52,032
Most of the principal Chinese
merchants and towkays were there,

135
00:08:52,112 --> 00:08:56,197
along with a considerable number
of Arab residents, and the paper

136
00:08:56,277 --> 00:09:00,250
called it "the largest which has
ever been held in Singapore".

137
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:03,893
The meeting, chaired by
the merchant John Anderson,

138
00:09:03,973 --> 00:09:07,550
passed its resolutions of
protest by acclamation.

139
00:09:07,550 --> 00:09:11,800
Among the speakers were Tan
Keong Saik and Lee Keng Yan.

140
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:16,152
Looking back in 1895, the Straits
Times summed up the resolutions

141
00:09:16,232 --> 00:09:20,993
as a protest against the "squeezing
of the life-blood of our prosperity

142
00:09:21,073 --> 00:09:25,084
and development" and against
"the barren honour of being the

143
00:09:25,164 --> 00:09:29,800
military guardians of Imperial
interests in the Straits of Malacca."

144
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:32,256
The protests did not stop the demand,

145
00:09:32,336 --> 00:09:35,900
and the fall in the value
of silver made it heavier.

146
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:39,055
The contribution was
set in pounds sterling,

147
00:09:39,135 --> 00:09:43,325
but the colony's revenue was
collected in silver dollars.

148
00:09:43,325 --> 00:09:47,978
In 1891 a dollar was worth
three shillings and threepence,

149
00:09:48,058 --> 00:09:52,628
and by January 1895 it was
worth less than two shillings,

150
00:09:52,708 --> 00:09:56,625
so the same sum in pounds
cost far more dollars.

151
00:09:56,625 --> 00:10:01,722
By then London had settled
on £110,000 for 1897

152
00:10:01,802 --> 00:10:06,678
and £120,000 for 1898,
with a rise of £10,000

153
00:10:06,758 --> 00:10:11,083
a year after that until the contribution

154
00:10:11,163 --> 00:10:16,450
covered the full cost of
the garrison, £136,000.

155
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:21,827
In protest, most of the unofficial members
of the Legislative Council resigned,

156
00:10:21,907 --> 00:10:24,521
and the Singapore justices of the peace

157
00:10:24,601 --> 00:10:28,400
and the members of the Chinese
Advisory Board followed.

158
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:32,776
The dispute was settled in
1896, when the contribution

159
00:10:32,856 --> 00:10:36,900
was fixed at 20 per cent
of the colony's revenue.

160
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:40,150
The contribution did not get smaller.

161
00:10:40,150 --> 00:10:43,586
In 1914, according to the
National Library Board,

162
00:10:43,666 --> 00:10:47,748
the Straits Settlements paid
the largest share of military

163
00:10:47,828 --> 00:10:51,479
funds to the Imperial
Exchequer of any Crown colony,

164
00:10:51,559 --> 00:10:55,075
and more than half of it
came from opium revenue.

165
00:10:55,075 --> 00:10:58,200
The map shows where each fort stood.

166
00:10:58,200 --> 00:11:01,043
Use the buttons to step
through the stages,

167
00:11:01,123 --> 00:11:04,375
from the two early batteries
in town to the forts

168
00:11:04,455 --> 00:11:07,650
guarding New Harbour,
and then to what is left.

169
00:11:07,650 --> 00:11:11,445
The gun emplacements left
at the old Fort Fullerton

170
00:11:11,525 --> 00:11:15,243
site were destroyed in
1889, the Post Office there

171
00:11:15,323 --> 00:11:18,814
was replaced by a larger
building in the 1920s,

172
00:11:18,894 --> 00:11:23,300
and that building, the Fullerton
Building, opened in 1928.

173
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:26,400
It is now the Fullerton Hotel.

174
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:30,075
Mount Palmer went next,
and the hill went with it.

175
00:11:30,075 --> 00:11:33,930
The fort was demolished in
1905 to make way for the

176
00:11:34,010 --> 00:11:38,174
second Telok Ayer reclamation
and the Telok Ayer Basin,

177
00:11:38,254 --> 00:11:42,110
and by the time that work
was finished in 1932 most

178
00:11:42,190 --> 00:11:46,125
of the hill had been dug
away and used as landfill.

179
00:11:46,125 --> 00:11:49,083
A small knoll with the
shrine of Habib Noh,

180
00:11:49,163 --> 00:11:54,381
a nineteenth-century Muslim saint, is
all that remains of it, beside a road

181
00:11:54,461 --> 00:11:58,700
still called Palmer Road, after
the old Palmer Battery Road.

182
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:01,658
Fort Canning was demolished in 1907,

183
00:12:01,738 --> 00:12:06,125
never having been used in
the defence of the colony.

184
00:12:06,125 --> 00:12:09,681
In the 1920s a large covered
reservoir for the city

185
00:12:09,761 --> 00:12:13,388
was built on the hilltop,
and the British Army later

186
00:12:13,468 --> 00:12:17,024
built an underground command
centre, the Battlebox,

187
00:12:17,104 --> 00:12:21,025
into the hill, which is the
subject of an earlier post.

188
00:12:21,025 --> 00:12:25,784
The fort's 1859 gateway and
some of its walls still stand,

189
00:12:25,864 --> 00:12:28,700
along with two nine-pound cannons.

190
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:32,750
The forts guarding New
Harbour lasted longest.

191
00:12:32,750 --> 00:12:36,857
Fort Pasir Panjang was
enlarged in 1892 into a

192
00:12:36,937 --> 00:12:41,863
six-gun battery with underground
magazines and tunnels,

193
00:12:41,943 --> 00:12:45,675
and in 1938 it was given two 6-inch guns.

194
00:12:45,675 --> 00:12:50,474
In February 1942, according
to the National Library Board,

195
00:12:50,554 --> 00:12:54,092
its guns were turned
almost 180 degrees and

196
00:12:54,172 --> 00:12:58,046
used by the Malay Regiment
in its 48-hour fight

197
00:12:58,126 --> 00:13:02,500
against the Japanese 18th
Division at Pasir Panjang.

198
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:06,385
The guns at Fort Siloso
were also turned inland and

199
00:13:06,465 --> 00:13:10,275
fired at Japanese troops
advancing from the west.

200
00:13:10,275 --> 00:13:13,598
After the war the tunnels at Labrador were

201
00:13:13,678 --> 00:13:18,136
forgotten until park officials
came across them in 2001,

202
00:13:18,216 --> 00:13:21,700
and they were opened
to the public in 2005.

203
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:25,804
Fort Siloso, on Sentosa,
is today a military museum

204
00:13:25,884 --> 00:13:29,575
and the only complete
fort left in Singapore.

205
00:13:29,575 --> 00:13:33,209
The question that filled
the Town Hall in 1891 was

206
00:13:33,289 --> 00:13:37,300
who should pay to defend a
trading port, and how much.

207
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:42,550
Today Singapore pays for its own
defence from its national budget.

208
00:13:42,550 --> 00:13:46,993
The Ministry of Defence describes
a policy of steady spending,

209
00:13:47,073 --> 00:13:50,641
projected to grow by about
3 to 4 per cent a year,

210
00:13:50,721 --> 00:13:54,435
which it says avoids "the
disruptive changes arising

211
00:13:54,515 --> 00:13:58,600
from fluctuations in the
expenditure from year to year".

212
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:02,544
That is a different arrangement
from the sudden demands

213
00:14:02,624 --> 00:14:06,575
from London that the merchants
of the 1890s protested.

214
00:14:06,575 --> 00:14:09,799
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Fort Fullerton,

215
00:14:09,879 --> 00:14:14,557
Fort Palmer and the fort on Fort
Canning Hill gave their names to places

216
00:14:14,637 --> 00:14:18,390
most Singaporeans pass every
day, from the Fullerton Hotel

217
00:14:18,470 --> 00:14:22,223
to Palmer Road, but the forts
themselves were removed more

218
00:14:22,303 --> 00:14:26,400
than a century ago and rarely
fired a shot except in practice.

219
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:29,450
The harder fight was over the bill.

220
00:14:29,450 --> 00:14:32,952
A free port that taxed none
of its trade paid for its

221
00:14:33,032 --> 00:14:37,143
garrison in large part out of
opium revenue, and its merchants

222
00:14:37,223 --> 00:14:41,075
packed the Town Hall to argue
that the bill was too high.
